A||||5||gabriel||3800SC & 4T65E question  - Terryk?||||6||
Z||000000||gabriel||02-07-2001||02:46 PM|||| Hi everyone, I am new to the forum.<BR>(and new to Fieros!)<P>Now I've looked this up in the archives, but I just want to clarify a thing or two,<P> What's the diff between the 4T60/E & the 4T65/E insofar as swapping into a Fiero?<BR>(w/3800SC)<BR>I have a auto 2m6 for an auto 2 auto swap. although I'm really considering tossing the 3800 into a manual (getrag) setup instead, and perhaps using an aftermarket ECM if I can't resolve the issues with the stock ECM.  Has anyone here used one of those?<P>Any issues here?  I think I understand the 4T60/E axle swaps.<BR>The best price I can find for the 3800SC series II & 4T65E combo (complete w/harness, accessories & brainbox) is $1850 delivered.  Seem okay?  The engine is pretty low miles & the donor car was driven into the yard.<P> Thanks in advance forr your time.<BR>BTW, I think this forum is pretty D**m great.<BR>There's an incredible amount of tech. knowledge base here!||||reg||6
Z||000001||terryk||02-07-2001||04:06 PM||||Physically, not much difference. Same axles, mounting, etc. However, both are electronically controlled with the 4T65E having the additional shift quality control. You won't be using an aftermarket ECM with either. Other than the shift quality control and the larger TC, they are essentially the same trans performance and rating wise.<P>I'd avoid the aftermarket ECM at all costs. Stick with the one that comes with the engine or at least swap to one of the other year ECM's.<P>$1850 is a good price if the miles are low. Make sure it includes the PCM, wiring harness, about 2ft of the fuel lines, the pipe from the rear manifold to the cat, the air filter box and purge canister. <P>Also, the 96 Series II/4T60EHD combo will run on the 94/95 ECM. That has a removable mem-cal rather than being a flash PCM. Ergo, any Series II engine will run on that ECM. It won't work with a 4T65E though. If you get a 65E great (you must run the matching PCM), but don't get hung up on it. A 60EHD is quite nice and offers more flexibility.<P>As for a manual trans, well, you know the issues.<P>Good luck!<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 02-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||gabriel||02-07-2001||05:29 PM||||Thanks Ter.<P>  Now for a dumb question:  I keep seeing references to PCM.  Okay, is this different from the ECM?  <P>  Yeah, everything that I want can be had, as the engine hasn't been pulled yet.  Good tips on the fuel line & canisters & such.<BR>I'll get them all.<BR> <BR>  Anyone have thoughts on the performance differance betwixt manual & auto in this particular application?<P>  And I have heard that the 86 buick chip was hacked, but has it been really?  Just trying to get around that RPM limiter thing ... (not that I see a use to do 120mph+ - just that limitations bug me)<BR>I don't have the donor car info in front of me, so I'm not sure which year this combo is from - it's either a 96 or 97 - (did a LOT of searching), but I'll get it in the am.  Again, thanks!<P>  Any one else out there just dying to input?<BR>Open to recieve!<P>||||reg||14
Z||000003||terryk||02-07-2001||05:41 PM||||ECM is Engine Control Module<BR>PCM is Powertrain Control Module<P>People use PCM to distinguish a flash programmable controller from a mem-cal controller although that's not the real difference between the boxes (which is a long explanation). It's just habit now. The main point is that to change the calibrations in an ECM, there is a removable module that is reprogrammed. On a PCM they are changed by connecting them to another box that changes the contents.<P>Tons of the applications have been hacked (86 Buick Turbo is but one) but the flash PCM's are still being worked on except for the few GM engineers that reprogram them on the side for big bucks. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||gabriel||02-08-2001||10:59 AM||||Hi.<P>   It's from a 97 Olds 88 LSS.<P> Got the ex.pipe, air cleaner box & purge can - cost another $150  grumble, grumble..<P> I see that you ordered your cradle from chriswest.  Like it?  Can't the fiero cradle be modified instead?<P>  So, how many members own the 3800?<P>Think I'll see about some engine mods before I start to install ... - hehe<P> Oh, to clarify.  The PCM is not a separate item from the ECM, it's just the designation of a different type of module, correct?<P> Let me know if I'm asking too many questions!||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||02-08-2001||12:01 PM||||Without getting into boring detail you can consider an ECM and PCM to be the same thing. They control the engine, trans and cruise. I'll call them all ECM from now on.<P>Chris's bracket works fine. Yes, you could mod the cradle but you will still need a crank mount. The 3800 A-car bracket should work, but the cradle will need modding.||||reg||1
